Prerequisites
-------------

To build the libraries you need

  - Meson (at least version 1.1; https://mesonbuild.com/) and Ninja

  - A C++ compiler supporting C++23

  - Boost (at least the filesystem library; http://www.boost.org/)

  - GNU gettext for the translations

Parts of this libraries need

  - Gtkmm 4 (which itself needs GTK 4). Get if from http://www.gtkmm.org/.

  - libgtkhtml: Part of GNOME (http://www.gnome.org/)

  - GtkMozEmbed: A GTK+ widget embedding Mozilla (part of Firefox or
    Seamonkey); Get it from http://www.mozilla.org/

  - A CORBA implementation (tested with MICO 2.3.9 (http://www.mico.org/))

  - doxygen to generate the documentation (available from
    http://www.doxygen.org/)


The package will compile without them, but you don't get the whole
functionality (not surprisingly the CORBA- and/or the X-windows
library are missing). The libgtkhtml and GtkMozEmbed are used to
display the help of (X-)applications (load faster as a full browser).


How to build
------------

Inside the directory containing the source code type

  meson setup build [options]     # Configure into the directory build
  meson compile -C build          # Build
  meson test -C build             # Run some tests on (some) classes
  meson install -C build          # Install the whole thing


* Options

  Options are passed to "meson setup" as -D<option>=<value>, or changed
  later with "meson configure build -D<option>=<value>". Calling
  "meson configure build" without further arguments lists all options with
  their current values.

  --prefix=<path>               # default: /usr/local
  -Dbuildtype=<type>            # plain, debug, debugoptimized (default)
                                  or release
  -Ddefault_library=shared      # Don't build static libs (shared, static
                                  or both)
  -Db_ndebug=true               # Define NDEBUG (disables the Checks)
  -Dcheck_level=<level>         # Adds asserts and Checks (default: -1 = off)
  -Dtrace_level=<level>         # Sets tracelevel (default: -1 = off)
  -Dnls=disabled                # Disable national language support
  -Dxgp=disabled|enabled        # Don't build/force building libXGP
                                  (default: build it, if gtkmm is found)
  -Dgmp=enabled                 # Use GNU's multiple precision library
  -Dnetwork_traversing=true     # Adds classes to traverse directories over
                                  a network

  The compiler and its flags can be set through the environment when
  running "meson setup" for the first time, e.g.:

       CXX=clang++ CXXFLAGS=-march=native meson setup build

  Several build directories (e.g. with different options) can be used at
  the same time.


* Additional targets (meson compile -C build <target>)

  - X-Appl                      # The sample application of libXGP
  - doc                         # Creates the documentation in the doc
                                  subdirectory (needs doxygen)
  - libYGP-pot                  # Updates the message template po/libYGP.pot
  - libYGP-update-po            # Merges the template into the translations


* Uninstall

  ninja -C build uninstall      # Uninstalls (after you found out what crap
                                  this package actually contains)
